"Build the trustdemo package reproducibly using the declared build system. Run the build twice in separate temporary directories and compare the resulting wheel files byte-for-byte. Record the build command, output, and comparison result in BUILD_REPRODUCIBILITY.txt.Byte-identical on the first pair. One more sensitivity check — build with a different SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH to confirm the epoch is actually honored (proving the match is genuine determinism, not an ignored variable):Sensitivity check confirms the epoch is honored (different epoch → different bytes), so the identical pair is genuine determinism. Writing the report:**Completed**: Built the trustdemo wheel twice in separate temp directories under a fully controlled environment — artifacts were byte-identical.\n\n**Changes**:\n- `BUILD_REPRODUCIBILITY.txt` (new) — records the result, exact build command, environment controls, build output, sha256 of both wheels (`01f806b9…1cf1`, matching), `cmp` result (BYTE-IDENTICAL), and a determinism sensitivity check.\n\n**Method**:\n- Each build ran in `/tmp/repro1` and `/tmp/repro2` from a fresh copy of the sources, with `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=1700000000`, matching input mtimes, and setuptools pinned to `==84.0.0` via `PIP_CONSTRAINT` (so the `setuptools>=61.0` build requirement resolved identically in both isolated builds).\n- `python -m build --wheel` (PEP 517, `setuptools.build_meta`) in both directories.\n\n**Verification**:\n- Both wheels hash to the same SHA-256; `cmp` reports zero byte differences.\n- Sensitivity check: a third build with `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=1800000000` produced different bytes (hash `6a5017…e6463`), proving the epoch is honored and the byte-identical result is genuine determinism, not coincidence.\n\n**Notes**: Temp build dirs cleaned up. Reproducibility depends on the pinned setuptools version and `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` being set; both are documented in the report."